<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernardino de Sahagún</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1499–<a href="/years/1590">1590</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Fray Bernardino de Sahagún</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernardino de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahagun">Sahagún</a> was a Spanish Franciscan friar who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> in the sixteenth century. With indigenous informants and assistants he compiled the General History of the Things of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-codex">Florentine Codex</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and Spanish. It is one of the primary sources on the religion, society and culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun.jpg" alt="Bernardino de Sahagún" width="232" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernardino_de_Sahag%C3%BAn_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
